{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/apphost at exact clean pushed head 3f431a32a353c2d6078791add1cfbb262df12f04 on request/create-config-replacement-safety, answering 4f38749f. This is the implementation report for that request.\n\nWhat the defect was. CreateConfig created the configuration at its destination and, when the write or close failed, removed it again behind an os.SameFile guard. That guard read equal device and inode numbers as proof the destination still held this call's own file. An inode number is not a durable identity: Linux frees and reuses it eagerly, so once the partial file was unlinked, a configuration another process had stored at that path could answer as this call's file and the cleanup deleted it. That is GitHub run 32670745698, and the defect is mine, from the configuration custody merge d4f54392.\n\nWhat the head does. Creation writes and closes a staging file at a unique private name in the same directory, then hard-links it to the destination. The link is refused with os.ErrExist when any entry already sits there, so of two concurrent creators exactly one wins, a stored genesis is never overwritten, and a concurrent reader never sees partial content. Every path - success, write failure, close failure, link refusal - removes exactly the staging file, whose name no other process holds. There is no identity to judge, so the class of defect is closed by construction rather than by a better comparison.\n\nConditions of 4f38749f, each against what was actually done.\n\nReproduce run 32670745698: reproduced on a real Ubuntu ext4 virtual machine. ext4 handed back the freed inode number, and the pre-fix code failed there with \"the replacement was removed by cleanup\". APFS never reuses the number, which is why the failure was invisible on this host.\n\nCleanup targets only the created file, and does not depend on os.SameFile: os.SameFile and both identity seams are gone from production code; the only remaining mentions are comments explaining why inode equality was never identity. createConfigRemove has one call site and is only ever reached with the staging name.\n\nDeterministic Linux-sensitive mutation tests: twelve mutations, each confirmed applied by diff and confirmed to compile before measurement, with no survivors and no non-measurements. Two matter most and were proved fail-first against the previous test file, where both survived: deleting the os.ErrClosed guard on the fallback close, and storing a different but valid genesis. Each is now killed by exactly one new assertion.\n\nGates: gofmt clean, git diff --check clean, go vet, GOOS=windows go build, go test -race over internal/apphost and internal/app, and go test ./... -count=1 across all thirty packages. All exit zero, no failures, nothing cached.\n\nWhat this head does not prove, stated so no reviewer has to discover it.\n\nTestCreateConfigLeavesAReplacementAtItsDestinationAlone passes against the pre-fix code on APFS, so on this host it does not pin the defect it is named for. Its comment now says so. The platform-independent guard is TestCreateConfigCleanupRemovesOnlyWhatItCreated, which fails against the pre-fix code everywhere. The replacement test earns its place for a different reason: it is the only test that kills a mutation removing the destination directly, bypassing the seam.\n\nA filesystem that cannot hard-link refuses creation with no fallback. That is read from the code - os.Link has one call site, its error is returned unchanged, and there is no rename fallback, error-class branching or retry - not measured on such a filesystem, because none was mountable here. The consequence is that attach can no longer succeed where gs init still can. cf21240e3e assigns documenting that boundary, and I have promised it.\n\nWindows is compile-checked only; no Windows host ran the tests.\n\nNothing fsyncs the staging file or the directory before the link, so a crash can persist the directory entry ahead of the data. The doc comment now scopes its \"absent or complete\" claim to concurrent readers. This is not a regression: the previous code left a partial file at the destination in the same circumstance, and WriteFileAtomically does not fsync either.\n\nA staging file orphaned by a crash is never cleaned up, because nothing in production enumerates the directory. It is inert, mode 0600, and does not occupy the destination. WriteFileAtomically already leaks the same way, so this head sets no new standard.\n\nIf the link succeeds but the staging removal then fails, the configuration is stored and CreateConfig still returns an error, which AttachConfig treats as fatal because it filters only os.ErrExist. A retry succeeds through the os.Stat that precedes creation. The error text says the configuration was stored.\n\nSimplification. An independent pre-review found the head duplicated the shape of WriteFileAtomically at six times the size. Because every path removes exactly the staging file, abandonStagedConfig and removeStagedConfig collapsed into a single removal site that chooses between the two existing messages. Three functions became two. The fallback-close seam was kept deliberately: merging it into the close seam would need a call-counting closure and make it ambiguous which close failed.\n\nArchitecture. internal/apphost and its only consumer internal/app. The custody contract at docs/reference/architecture.md 316-337 is preserved, not changed: exclusive creation, no fallback, and a reader that fails closed all still hold, and no page in docs/reference describes CreateConfig's mechanism. No contract change, so no architecture.md candidate accompanies this head.","body":{"commit":"3f431a32a353c2d6078791add1cfbb262df12f04","path":"internal/apphost"}}